---
/**
 * Home page — the single page for v1 (OVERVIEW.md §2.3).
 * Composes the six content sections in order inside one <main> landmark, with
 * the Footer as a sibling of <main> rather than its last child (ADR-0004: a
 * footer nested in main never maps to the `contentinfo` landmark; phase 0's
 * "six components inside one main" criterion is superseded for Footer).
 *
 * Rendered through Base.astro. Container styling consumes tokens only; section
 * internals are styled by each component's own scoped styles. Footer.astro
 * repeats these container rules for itself — Astro scopes styles to the
 * elements in their own template, so the `main` rule below cannot reach a
 * sibling component's root element.
 *
 * --- The reveal system (ADR-0005) ---------------------------------------
 *
 * This page owns BOTH halves of the site's scroll-triggered motion: the one
 * inline script permitted by ADR-0005, and the CSS contract that script
 * drives. They live together deliberately — the script is meaningless without
 * the rules keyed off the class it adds, and splitting them would leave the
 * budget-capped script in one file and its only observable effect in another.
 *
 * The contract, in three parts:
 *
 *   1. A section opts in by putting `data-reveal` on its root element. The
 *      observer watches every such element and adds `is-revealed` once, then
 *      unobserves — the reveal plays once and never re-arms.
 *   2. Inside an opted-in section, elements carrying `.reveal` animate. The
 *      class is an explicit opt-in rather than a `> *` descendant sweep so a
 *      section can animate its own internals on its own terms: Protocol
 *      already staggers `.state`/`.loop` off `is-revealed`, and a generic
 *      child rule would fade the whole `<ol>` in on top of that.
 *   3. Stagger is `--reveal-delay`, set by the owning component on its own
 *      `.reveal` elements. Components choreograph; this page only supplies
 *      the mechanism.
 *
 * Why `is:global` rather than global.css: the mechanism belongs next to the
 * script that drives it, and this slice's declared scope does not include
 * src/styles/global.css. If a later slice grows a second consumer of the
 * reveal system outside this page, that is the moment to promote it.
 *
 * Never-invisible rule (ADR-0005). The animation is applied ONLY when
 * `is-revealed` is present AND motion is not reduced. Nothing anywhere sets a
 * hidden base state, so with JS off, with the observer unsupported, or under
 * `prefers-reduced-motion: reduce`, every section renders complete and
 * immediately — the class adds motion, never visibility. `backwards` fill is
 * what makes that possible: it holds the start frame through the stagger
 * delay, so the delay needs no `opacity: 0` base to hide behind.
 *
 * The script is `is:inline` because ADR-0005 mandates an *inline* observer; a
 * processed <script> would be bundled to an external module. is:inline is
 * passed through verbatim and never minified, so it is written hand-minified
 * on one line, and it is placed after the content so the elements it queries
 * exist. Budget: ≤ 1024 bytes in dist (ADR-0005 gate).
 *
 * Options: no `threshold`, because intersectionRatio is a fraction of the
 * target's OWN height and a section taller than the viewport could never
 * reach a fractional threshold on a narrow phone (the trap phase-1-ws-1.2
 * documented for the diagram). Instead the default 0 threshold is pulled in
 * by a -10% bottom `rootMargin`, so a section arms once its top edge is a
 * little way into the viewport rather than the instant it grazes the fold.
 * One consequence, stated so it is not a discovery: a section shorter than
 * 10% of the viewport height sitting flush at the very bottom of the page
 * could never intersect the shrunk root. None of the five opted-in sections
 * is anywhere near that short, and the last of them (Install) is followed by
 * the footer, so none sits flush at the bottom.
 *
 * This rationale lives in the frontmatter, not in an HTML comment beside the
 * script: frontmatter comments are compiled away, while an HTML comment
 * naming a script element literally would survive into dist and break the
 * phase gate's "exactly one script element in dist/index.html" count.
 */
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import Hero from "../components/Hero.astro";
import Problem from "../components/Problem.astro";
import Personas from "../components/Personas.astro";
import Protocol from "../components/Protocol.astro";
import Workflow from "../components/Workflow.astro";
import Install from "../components/Install.astro";
import Footer from "../components/Footer.astro";

const title = "metis — the meta-harness for AI coding agents";
const description =
  "metis governs AI coding agents with a disciplined protocol: one slice at a time, file scope as a contract, and cross-vendor review — so scope creep, self-review, and lost context stop happening.";
---

<Base title={title} description={description}>
  <main>
    <Hero />
    <Problem />
    <Personas />
    <Protocol />
    <Workflow />
    <Install />
  </main>
  <Footer />
  <script is:inline>const o=new IntersectionObserver((es,ob)=>es.forEach(e=>{if(e.isIntersecting){e.target.classList.add("is-revealed");ob.unobserve(e.target)}}),{rootMargin:"0px 0px -10% 0px"});document.querySelectorAll("[data-reveal]").forEach(t=>o.observe(t))</script>
</Base>

<style>
  main {
    max-width: var(--container-max);
    margin-inline: auto;
    padding-inline: var(--space-4);
  }
</style>

<style is:global>
  /* --- Reveal system (ADR-0005) -----------------------------------------
     Unscoped on purpose: these rules have to reach `.reveal` elements
     rendered by the section components, which Astro's scoped selectors
     cannot do from a page. See the frontmatter for the full contract.

     300ms (--duration-base) sits at the top of the 200-300ms envelope
     OVERVIEW §3.2 specifies for section reveals, and inside ADR-0002's
     wider 200-700ms one. 16px (--space-3) is the maximum translate the
     plan allows. Nothing loops: one iteration, `backwards` fill, no
     `forwards` — the animation ends on the same frame the base rules
     already render. */
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) {
    [data-reveal].is-revealed .reveal {
      animation: reveal-in var(--duration-base) var(--ease-out) backwards;
      animation-delay: var(--reveal-delay, 0ms);
    }
  }

  /* Global by necessity, not by choice: Astro does not scope @keyframes
     names even inside a scoped block (the built CSS keeps `state-in`
     verbatim), so four per-component copies of this would be four
     colliding global definitions of the same name. One definition, in the
     one file that owns the mechanism. */
  @keyframes reveal-in {
    from {
      opacity: 0;
      transform: translateY(var(--space-3));
    }
    to {
      opacity: 1;
      transform: none;
    }
  }
</style>
